.08, "key_factors": ["Open seat with Angie Craig retiring removes incumbent advantage in a D+3 district", "Expert ratings cluster at Likely/Safe D across ten raters", "Prediction markets price Little near 92% to win", "Little out-raised Pratt and holds an outside-spending edge"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point Democratic: a D+3 district, a favorable national environment, and a September Democratic-sponsored poll showing Little up twelve. Markets and most raters treat this as beyond reach for Pratt, though the open seat and thin polling argue for a somewhat narrower expected margin than the safest ratings imply.", "watch": "A late shift in the national House environment toward Republicans, or a strong Pratt debate performance, could tighten this into genuine competition.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Angie Craig (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's 2nd District is an open seat after Democrat Angie Craig left to run for Senate, removing the incumbent advantage that has helped hold this D+3 district. Matt Little, who won a contested DFL primary in August, faces Republican Eric Pratt. Little has raised more than twice Pratt's total and benefits from outside spending on his side, while Pratt's cash position is modest.\n\nRatings lean Democratic across the board: several outlets call it Likely D, others Safe or Solid D, and prediction markets put Little's chances around 92%. The one recent poll, a Democratic-sponsored September survey of likely voters, showed Little ahead by twelve points. The forecast expects a Democratic win in the high single digits, with enough uncertainty to keep the race from being called safe given the open seat and limited independent polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9565, "margin": 11.37}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9477, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9516, "margin": 11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9571, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "